#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096619 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096419 :question/id #uuid "072ce609-b04d-57b0-9c7d-cbb8765c38fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3636" :question/problem "If a curve $\\mathcal C$ is given by $y=e^x\\sin x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\dfrac \\pi 2.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x) = e^x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = e^x,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos⁡x+sin⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\dfrac\\pi 2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi /2} & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(cos⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+sin⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi /2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $e^{\\pi/2}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711 :topic/math-academy-id 332 :topic/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a701631f-ee6f-5c74-8945-ae030b322172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095407} {:answer/id #uuid "b33ae4fd-7df5-5668-be3f-6da8b92a1f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095408} {:answer/id #uuid "e5c620ec-258c-57ed-8f58-6c1af87e31f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095409} {:answer/id #uuid "30ae4bc2-f079-544b-81c3-abc3d5c10ec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095410} {:answer/id #uuid "497ee82d-da93-5f82-adc3-3cb7e1142e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095411}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d50d9198-c7df-55ec-ba39-bff28ab4846e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095406}] :question/id #uuid "544be85e-c712-5922-8992-f82de75c2029" :question/math-academy-id "q-48391" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y = 2e^{x}(x + 1)$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x = 0$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c7eb625-3ff4-5e82-bd70-a085123a70c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3π^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095485} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b825b6-1cae-5e24-9e36-665ccf694b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6π^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095486} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab1f0af-b2c2-5a25-97d0-1ea8b6003204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3π^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095487} {:answer/id #uuid "5e676af8-cf84-5f73-998c-145d20246a1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095488} {:answer/id #uuid "73d74933-2b73-5e41-8a53-45938d45db82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6π^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095489}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b2dfab29-1db5-50bf-875c-fe928ce84d34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095484}] :question/id #uuid "1feafbad-2c59-53b5-8e99-0aba4e213ff3" :question/math-academy-id "q-48901" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y = 8x^{3}\\sin x$, calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x = \\frac{π}{2}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233776 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d79a7bd8-521e-5538-bc94-8c3a64e6621d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31226" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{tan}x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6x,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={sec}^{2}x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2})({sec}^{2}x)+(\\operatorname{tan}x)(6x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}x+6x\\operatorname{tan}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{x=\\pi } & =3{x}^{2}{sec}^{2}x+6x\\operatorname{tan}x{|}_{x=\\pi } \\\\  & =3(\\pi )^{2}{sec}^{2}(\\pi )+6(\\pi )\\operatorname{tan}(\\pi ) \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{2}\\cdot 1+6\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3{\\pi }^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233814 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9bc784-4648-528a-ac26-6d3516a71384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232589}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b9bc784-4648-528a-ac26-6d3516a71384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232589} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a2fdd90-ea5c-5dc2-bea7-c9bda09ae803" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232588}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c831400-ba54-50fb-91a6-b9b14e55efca" :question/math-academy-id "q-319825" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{2}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x-1)}{2x+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =\\frac{2(0-1)}{2(0)+1}+\\operatorname{ln}(2(0)+1) \\\\  & =(-2)+\\operatorname{ln}(1) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235431 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ead734d2-e970-5e92-9ac3-1260bbfab982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ead734d2-e970-5e92-9ac3-1260bbfab982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234918} :answer-field/id #uuid "3429cda5-daf9-53dc-9c18-72e4afbade0a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235025}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dabe687a-249f-595c-be70-5214c92992e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-167326" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbf988f2-8940-5547-8a0c-461e7fba4865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbf988f2-8940-5547-8a0c-461e7fba4865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234891} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6fb57fa-80a8-5893-88ed-795c73c49674" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235177}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8af2c303-b3aa-5790-885e-7a978a609bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167780" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299797 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94fe4615-3627-5184-b596-faacf4579fe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94fe4615-3627-5184-b596-faacf4579fe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298195} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5b5855-e173-5cc0-be78-e8c7ed94613e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299155}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6dd1a867-f879-56d0-b4c0-d0297b5035d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-319806" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x+2)cos⁡x,$ then the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x+2$ and $v(x)=cos⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-sin⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x+2)\\cdot (-sin⁡x)+cos⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-(x+2)sin⁡x+cos⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)sin⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+cos⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)(1)+0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299798 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a9ff769-7920-54d9-9d56-bc88c33849af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a9ff769-7920-54d9-9d56-bc88c33849af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298084} :answer-field/id #uuid "4571a1bf-33ea-5e4c-ab47-61e0c018609f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298876}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6b71a24-90f7-5ca7-b521-491ff5b33ac3" :question/math-academy-id "q-319829" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(x-1)ln⁡(4x-3),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x-1$ and $v(x)=ln⁡(4x-3).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4}{4x-3}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{4x-3}+ln⁡(4x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{4}{4(1)-3}+ln⁡(4(1)-3) \\\\  & =0+ln⁡(1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bfce910-f1a6-55e0-aa0a-ef391e8972f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bfce910-f1a6-55e0-aa0a-ef391e8972f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298162} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b17fdea-2304-5379-9c2f-9f4ff86470d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298785}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dcf49dc9-80d7-5610-a247-cb1da3f12efe" :question/math-academy-id "q-319842" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=({x}^{2}-4x+7){e}^{x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=0$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{2}-4x+7$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x-4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4x+7)\\cdot {e}^{x}+{e}^{x}\\cdot (2x-4) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={{e}^{x}({x}^{2}-2x+3)|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (0-0+3)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329890 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d41e3a6-5106-5f1f-89a7-bdf0659fd667" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327605} {:answer/id #uuid "5722ae40-633d-5817-9b2f-fd696d396624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327690} {:answer/id #uuid "7d059a2c-54e1-581a-9631-274cba73cd9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328010} {:answer/id #uuid "e54916ae-cb46-54d5-9769-ffc8af47b71e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328941} {:answer/id #uuid "ef1b3eab-bf60-5779-ac14-8316a78be3d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5722ae40-633d-5817-9b2f-fd696d396624" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327690} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4c9edf-2978-575c-9b65-ca2b012c2b2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329326}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33d86109-fb1d-5bea-8ffd-bc18d4e7651a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48515" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=(3x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=3x-2$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =(3x-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3x-2}{x}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=1$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{3x-2}{x}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1)-2}{1}+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329891 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02bc770f-35b5-51f7-ac23-0dd7e7626849" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326966} {:answer/id #uuid "a57078c9-1989-5bc2-8b84-47cb7c7b7fce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328362} {:answer/id #uuid "acccd38a-7649-5753-9632-b00f2c05b4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328433} {:answer/id #uuid "bce8eacc-997c-5af0-a8a5-5af0dda6e688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328578} {:answer/id #uuid "d019d383-2037-503a-8480-3e5bdb84df98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi {e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acccd38a-7649-5753-9632-b00f2c05b4e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328433} :answer-field/id #uuid "8008b7a2-773f-5257-8a67-dd590b27a6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329395}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7dfe1e7-2a9e-5927-a9e6-a1ed58369b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-48516" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y={e}^{x}sin⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=sin⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot cos⁡x+(sin⁡x)\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & ={{e}^{x}(cos⁡x+sin⁡x)|}_{x=\\pi } \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-{e}^{\\pi }.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122dcace-6bca-59bb-b96f-a96026e9a833" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327088} {:answer/id #uuid "29771cef-6628-575d-b6d2-039fa89b2a35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327286} {:answer/id #uuid "3101f4e9-df22-5461-be7e-f6674a765dce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327368} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e4fd4c-0122-574c-8317-faf21984d650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328648} {:answer/id #uuid "e154d586-179b-57cc-acf3-bc95bc04c67c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3e4fd4c-0122-574c-8317-faf21984d650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328648} :answer-field/id #uuid "745d0171-7216-5576-96bb-4a33b2c2f9b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329378}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1066779d-ebe1-53a9-ad1f-5e9c5ee1f7e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-48517" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=({x}^{3}-1){e}^{x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)={x}^{3}-1$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =({x}^{3}-1)\\cdot {e}^{x}+{e}^{x}\\cdot (3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=2$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=2} & ={{e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-1)|}_{x=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}({2}^{3}+3(2)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =19{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $19{e}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fb89b95-0bba-5b10-8fe3-527b548e1601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327074} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5aa1e3-21ee-5889-8055-e0735761bea1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328124} {:answer/id #uuid "c4cf7b66-0f01-5eca-acc9-cb3a3d67bb13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328659} {:answer/id #uuid "d10f42e2-911f-5fb9-b0b0-c03a2475a986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328770} {:answer/id #uuid "e360ba04-1df2-5392-a2fb-baf0e0e1b838" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fb89b95-0bba-5b10-8fe3-527b548e1601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327074} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7bf395d-b055-550a-8f21-50f7bb4a70c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329507}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a6705a9-7a56-57af-9eab-fa453a263686" :question/math-academy-id "q-48993" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=e.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=e$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=e} & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡e \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Product  at a Given Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096648 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096445 :question/id #uuid "069b2882-f4a9-589f-a903-4e89d077cd85" :question/math-academy-id "e-4279" :question/problem "Suppose, $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\neq \\mathbf{0}$. Given that $7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\lambda \\left(3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b} \\right)$ are equal vectors, find the scalar factor $\\lambda$." :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n$$\n7\\mathbf{a}+14\\mathbf{b}= \\dfrac{7}{3} (3\\mathbf{a}+6\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda=\\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d5c96d-ca13-5c1e-83d3-0dc9f6da0f83" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051079 :topic/math-academy-id 87 :topic/title "Factoring Linear Expressions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097030 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9baa3219-e5ec-524d-ad2c-2cbd42340400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094154} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb4a605-3985-519c-8727-624a59fc1822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094155} {:answer/id #uuid "126be9dc-c4b5-539e-a882-43d7b90deeee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $λ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094156} {:answer/id #uuid "4c760e90-e2a0-509b-8ac9-fbffc078956e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094157} {:answer/id #uuid "64856629-464b-597b-b654-8ec83e0093e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094158}] :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce04e39-dab9-503a-8d12-65dab19c0d4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094153}] :question/id #uuid "89e3c8db-28b5-569c-9a44-fd5145785013" :question/math-academy-id "q-12135" :question/problem "Given that $4a - 12b$ and $λ(a - 3b)$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a16749e0-6e32-5eba-9b79-9729809c39b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094160} {:answer/id #uuid "f1646b29-68ea-5559-817f-27ea925c247e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094161} {:answer/id #uuid "0e04de2e-1e6f-53de-9812-661940aeeb5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No such $λ$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094162} {:answer/id #uuid "2f323117-6a87-5268-98a2-88222812dded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094163} {:answer/id #uuid "3affc5be-fa94-54f4-9c4c-3ec5eb031868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094164}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f488ca54-03f7-50ec-9161-6a8e2085e6d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094159}] :question/id #uuid "6d9ea524-5a88-54cd-80c7-a2443343a3f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-12139" :question/problem "Given that $λ(-0.5a + 6b)$ and $a - 12b$ are equal (non-zero) vectors, find the scale factor $λ$."}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Vectors are Parallel"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096649 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096446 :question/id #uuid "d1329498-4413-5d87-a814-be0d57a3afd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-4280" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf a$ is shown in the diagram below. Draw the vectors $2\\mathbf a$ and $-3\\mathbf a$.\n\n![](images/a5/a5eaa8baaf303ecbfa6c0fb207d6b19836cc90fc331e238cd473109f44fddf80.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that both $2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ must be parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, for the vector $2\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $2\\mathbf{a}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $2\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction since the factor $2$ is positive.\n\n![](images/41/41eba7e6c5bbe420418726bd45efa668dc6be5ff5144ba35d52b53a43925c333.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $-3\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3\\mathbf{a}$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-3\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-3$ is negative.\n\n![](images/ed/eda3f2572d98ef1789292ec6d35569487b32efa459673dcb95f8fdd0d1f4ae5b.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc49f831-8e1d-5fb4-8952-1dd4ee3bfa02" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049603 :topic/math-academy-id 1100 :topic/title "The Triangle Law for the Addition of Two Vectors"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096968 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84bcbe16-7536-5125-b103-ef9c458c9db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d6c501b98e6eb5b998b6ebf822ec33e23dc1e67c01e3424e571ac19ded016a9a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093783} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d252a8-1a3a-531e-aba7-030ccb0e8aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fa/fafda2acdd5a0f4355abcb067afff9367a5cf10acbe0b22f976e165927c18968.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093784} {:answer/id #uuid "35f79dfb-e1f1-5ee4-86af-f6afc25792cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a1/a173c48fb3f47049dbf06ccaf4628313b367bdc0b7b2c18bd7a88a7ed33a2069.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093785} {:answer/id #uuid "38cf6707-6d85-5651-82d6-d2a22b325ed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0b/0b48a668105d698a5c055839f09d7a10c9b5b8da63bc05ca5a948bb6eb3df730.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093786} {:answer/id #uuid "748ec053-3c16-52b1-aa52-badc0e6e736e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d2/d230aec47e23f7e1c8b03eeb2487b2c59d83e33bb1901b55070dd5647777dfd7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093787}] :answer-field/id #uuid "282b29ca-f425-5dfd-bd27-5209be89cfc7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093782}] :question/id #uuid "7a4c088c-8d03-5a94-b71e-37db23fdd187" :question/math-academy-id "q-11324" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a89a9c2ff1cfd53a9d9e33c60591b482cbfa4044e313b2d2c031046bb21e5ce3.png)\n\nThe vector $a$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $3a$ and $-a$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097032 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8120675e-74e2-5aa5-8cbf-db2abc8f109e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/41/416a18fd3a2a11c7fbb0ce2fdd3ed75b83410396e8ec52b8a7c993745ca81b4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094166} {:answer/id #uuid "ed07a06b-80ba-50f5-89c6-e0a481b51456" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6c/6c907ff40a90580aad820b8ac461aed59fc55f1e98d5c79f56f60437fdf08890.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094167} {:answer/id #uuid "f4cd0334-67e8-5261-b4ce-8230e36aba22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c0/c03ee884a33c8edc202df3de774ab352649eaf3c319d0097c8fc1c9bc504b1aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094168} {:answer/id #uuid "6b191158-7808-5d59-bd26-046f3a9557c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5c/5cd9e52874ba6c0d9b72b309ff3223498f830d48eb1d43bdfbccdbc8c8cd6469.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094169} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef56eda-a107-5390-9358-102a190bd297" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e9/e95ebaf8e3be645cb7d75d5b473aab45841cbeb114f24a47e0f97af1f7c31db6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094170}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fa9fb757-41da-596f-8d49-c9a1b0447d8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094165}] :question/id #uuid "e2326efc-0c50-5a93-bc92-424305d03002" :question/math-academy-id "q-12140" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f54e51921dfd2fb1b93bccec89553e174e2527866352626a5bf0846956b096bb.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $a$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $-\\frac{1}{2}a$?"}] :knowledge-point/title "Creating Parallel Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096652 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096451 :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](images/b8/b8abf22d14f93a38c4d01f368b8171e4ccfe90086d932f5b58453baa46391405.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2n+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097165 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f77c7b4-45a3-55b8-b6d7-7134a1c5b198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285217} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd7c2f1-51ee-5686-91ae-4e42859d9003" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2.5$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285505} {:answer/id #uuid "847a4d81-38dd-55d5-a14b-b622eb3de871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286007} {:answer/id #uuid "ad470f62-fea2-533b-bd6f-9ee937e27815" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1.5$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286373} {:answer/id #uuid "dd53d27b-3e7e-58e0-8170-15ca40f8c8d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f77c7b4-45a3-55b8-b6d7-7134a1c5b198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285217} :answer-field/id #uuid "245a581a-aa5d-50db-805d-7bc423b9814c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287226}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b7aee39-22ab-5444-970d-797a67e85565" :question/math-academy-id "q-19370" :question/problem "![](images/a1/a1e251e00a99520cc392d031dbd05646ce07b65319d829e2cd8fc6f80e6e29a2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/60/601e049067de307ef2321ae9b516d1c7306d837ca0758c080074446a8c54389d.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=2a+2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1754eccd-ec7f-5204-806f-ed41f26894fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327138} {:answer/id #uuid "58b7e4a1-6805-552d-86f2-f1434646a625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =3.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327711} {:answer/id #uuid "63ebdee6-5ead-50c1-9a1a-f01abc2adffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327802} {:answer/id #uuid "a67826f4-23a8-5cfb-a73c-187b9a6eec4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328374} {:answer/id #uuid "f607dfd9-32a2-5e67-932b-093b37e029ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-2.5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a67826f4-23a8-5cfb-a73c-187b9a6eec4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328374} :answer-field/id #uuid "703a83ec-ece6-50c6-955a-a6ccb5003bf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329373}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6df187f-d344-54eb-95de-18be02ffa7f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-42670" :question/problem "![](images/01/01940c9fa894d635300a49867da35d483b873a45d2a75204c20c58e4cb84c883.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a0/a0d9fa1c366945e5e789b6361f929357af163db2e9be4a3ccc9023c12486aca6.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=2|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $r$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-2r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-2r-2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=-2r-2s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256375 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dcfec14-cb0a-5093-aca2-c64dcab4c730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253787} {:answer/id #uuid "1272e74e-fb78-59d2-9d84-d72aa670ad11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253830} {:answer/id #uuid "3e10a9f2-1e2c-5cf1-b294-748c37da198c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254206} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9b310f-087d-588a-be38-6cb7b7a187b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255489} {:answer/id #uuid "cebf7021-3b8a-5c16-b5a9-e42a4a2ed96f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1272e74e-fb78-59d2-9d84-d72aa670ad11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253830} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d68256d-13fe-57b1-b3cf-f8ae54b5b98f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255937}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf2e5cbf-66fb-56e7-95be-719efd096999" :question/math-academy-id "q-120268" :question/problem "![](images/25/25717d521feeec25f7b4b722ca7b8a4ee9b5fdc8423bac06953e5f2adc8eef3d.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d5/d585c949d7de8cc3fa2d62bfb60990af12242c32ef3390e563f7e627844c5a88.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=3|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ and $r$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=3r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =3r-2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=3r-2s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0956f584-e832-537c-9bfd-df2ed29f604c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253740} {:answer/id #uuid "8e097b9e-5e91-54a9-9371-58923e289845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254922} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ddd4ba-6c17-5bcf-8e2d-4f52eaf316fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255291} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1c43e3-09e0-524d-a0a4-f5fb8a9c819c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255483} {:answer/id #uuid "e2fc6d53-d302-54b7-88b6-3eff72e7c370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6ddd4ba-6c17-5bcf-8e2d-4f52eaf316fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255291} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a3dfd2-fcbc-54d2-80df-42dfa9700a6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256087}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "15e9fa4b-b469-5d54-bb3e-fd8c74e844aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-120277" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c991e8d6cc9cb869db0a0a663830f255f33dd32756167a5e665b2e4318ae7014.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a9/a9dcb3e3cd825cf24103f3636b79418531f8b8a14a5f6c83b9b94780fdf8739b.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=4|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=4p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =4p-2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=4p-2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3720d5ea-8c76-53f9-b41b-1454af520571" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254146} {:answer/id #uuid "61a80033-8bee-5b84-b56a-7cb802b9895a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254544} {:answer/id #uuid "649996fe-b9a2-51bc-9110-e9d71b6a1d00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254571} {:answer/id #uuid "af5d8017-d027-550d-b7c5-936f4606ab26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255212} {:answer/id #uuid "e69bcee0-66da-5af1-bec9-f709395b6bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af5d8017-d027-550d-b7c5-936f4606ab26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255212} :answer-field/id #uuid "e43afca9-d6cc-50ae-85ea-bc2853cb618f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256326}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "281f178c-d09e-5385-8ce5-3504549094a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-120279" :question/problem "![](images/fc/fc7d37da99173b7fa99f2f5f0bbf1478a54505628ed827488a18b3d01535f81f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/6c/6c9273c0b89d4c06c8a8591b8e8e94b0c83b2d1d4bd04b64ed8cb0a185619b5f.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=2|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2k.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-j-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-j-2k.$\n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4a9886-9cdf-527a-b227-543172b052f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253759} {:answer/id #uuid "2da17495-8691-5696-bd53-f949d4ef7fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254079} {:answer/id #uuid "5a69518f-7cb5-5ce5-9fdf-16cba2457c40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254465} {:answer/id #uuid "5b74da20-9621-50a3-8878-f3ac068c7f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254480} {:answer/id #uuid "ee39fd90-b83b-5bbc-9c9e-cb4fdc7476d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2da17495-8691-5696-bd53-f949d4ef7fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254079} :answer-field/id #uuid "9518d0fa-8d58-5ea0-85af-33850d0ea420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256195}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4d0ef3c-f0b3-5fa4-b071-4e8740b424bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-120281" :question/problem "![](images/41/41a40c03043152177c5bf6d7f56b51c09e57352480599b508d376018d87de1b3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, find $\\lambda $ and $\\mu .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cc/ccc52cbcbf17db97ad3849f157b5d6ab3e10f59846e2cd4b75507fc0a37c3c98.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3a-2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=-3a-2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292807 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e5ae413-be1a-5211-ad08-178e5e19be0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e5ae413-be1a-5211-ad08-178e5e19be0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291107} :answer-field/id #uuid "2daa9284-83e4-5449-8185-db49a6c674e4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291887} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d709123-c548-51c1-96cd-bc8ac660e378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d709123-c548-51c1-96cd-bc8ac660e378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291236} :answer-field/id #uuid "956ba8d7-fa47-5f7a-b145-9f5e1da669dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292202}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d447ec6b-433a-5b79-9c9d-143f856c643d" :question/math-academy-id "q-275128" :question/problem "![](images/99/992be4e67316804381ffc38860d2eaf23fd4a93cfe22ec4918c30c41e34ade57.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n\n$-\\frac{1}{2}$\n\nand \n$\\mu =$\n\n$-\\frac{5}{2}$\n\n." :question/worked-solution "![](images/c0/c0f7f4df52373ca602aff41c97a5b8413f510353874540b76147c527a2f4b18c.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=\\frac{1}{2}|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $r$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-\\frac{1}{2}r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=\\frac{5}{2}|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-\\frac{5}{2}s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}r-\\frac{5}{2}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=-\\frac{1}{2}r-\\frac{5}{2}s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{5}{2}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca2060e-cf6d-5017-97b3-a1a9cc42949a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ca2060e-cf6d-5017-97b3-a1a9cc42949a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299998} :answer-field/id #uuid "163edb16-7a87-5fe6-a6a3-1683f24ab220" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301297} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a31f4f1a-a6a7-58a5-9b89-d256ec3e6ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a31f4f1a-a6a7-58a5-9b89-d256ec3e6ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300785} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ddb2ab0-ae40-58ed-89e6-151fc2b146eb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301426}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8fc6062b-82cc-5523-a4f6-6f883f485c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-326013" :question/problem "![](images/58/586fb01cd796c9d4e04aaeb393ea7f5fadd6c836d0b39d36c9c668447c490bd1.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/2d/2d3a5c31af776cc49c2af118edc7256ca533320c7d53245593e5de6649ce03cf.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $u$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-u.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=2|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2v.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-u-2v.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v=-1u-2v.$\n\nSince $v∦u,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be2cc6e1-4dbf-5c23-8cb7-de5b9bae5093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be2cc6e1-4dbf-5c23-8cb7-de5b9bae5093" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300912} :answer-field/id #uuid "189a9488-2d59-5b59-aa06-4aec634d9205" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301307} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56dacd44-2964-5317-a14f-0fd91f5fc60f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56dacd44-2964-5317-a14f-0fd91f5fc60f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300415} :answer-field/id #uuid "75e05b55-bce1-5c1f-891f-1a5c2be6e59c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301592}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90a7567f-3548-5a15-bedd-9db11178758a" :question/math-academy-id "q-326014" :question/problem "![](images/de/de5905301a41f02237a48257a79e457f11ad74186942ca5bcf0002ab041aff91.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/1b/1be50e101b386f53ea5a2f1474f2e1d2debb8f98380d49a346dda32b37db70f1.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-2j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=\\frac{1}{2}|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nSince $k∦j,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{1}{2}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ff9eea8-3116-5c46-81b2-748e3985bd32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ff9eea8-3116-5c46-81b2-748e3985bd32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300195} :answer-field/id #uuid "13006d25-7abe-5d8d-9d49-2a380b810f7f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301287} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f65a6764-f098-5803-8dd2-cfae5e7bf3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f65a6764-f098-5803-8dd2-cfae5e7bf3cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301177} :answer-field/id #uuid "944773b9-99d6-5a0a-bf11-9eeecd9281a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301701}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52ee4dd6-7ab9-5429-b0f8-a112547f6aa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-326018" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c6dc7566d3ef4b0c047f048738aee6a204e0ba0e40cc48e83867456c6a1d67f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/56/563522ad9e5cf8e134a6669364986de592c7d63da244b050ef466e9f6c971a39.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =3p+2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=3p+2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302257 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c9290e7-f63b-5468-915b-34a0ae957945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c9290e7-f63b-5468-915b-34a0ae957945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300256} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a110ab2-a48d-53ec-a75a-46f122a37820" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301257} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9aacaac-a578-58c1-8401-72795e01f089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9aacaac-a578-58c1-8401-72795e01f089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300965} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d4ce281-3d77-51cf-9942-be3392d431c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301734}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2bbedc4-0ff6-53bb-b9ae-7bd7218983f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-326022" :question/problem "![](images/99/99d63eb31fba0757bde11dda3653e3791fcddb0b74b5325e20afcede4f3ea849.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda a+\\mu b$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/f7/f7acd51dcca9ad48f4aa7ec026beaeb67ddc9b1e88ab9b5042016674ba6ea542.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =a+4b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda a+\\mu b=1a+4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =4$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302315 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cad88663-74d7-54b2-94ad-a6d1774885ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300975}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cad88663-74d7-54b2-94ad-a6d1774885ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300975} :answer-field/id #uuid "48daf202-288f-5a92-909e-f4da6828eaf7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301472} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb5f7c80-490a-572c-a55e-314ae670f1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb5f7c80-490a-572c-a55e-314ae670f1f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301208} :answer-field/id #uuid "62599e82-fca2-5c45-9032-57359ac8e345" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301542}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1beeeaab-084b-5797-a939-fcb45aee542f" :question/math-academy-id "q-327074" :question/problem "![](images/c8/c8cbd5b93633e4e4899a1cde0da618f3be792bdbbd77db650c32814187b222f8.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/67/6708537b79154a7778fe52e8d82b6577f93d40a06d3af0267e3e4b7eaa6468a9.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OM=3|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $q$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-3q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=2|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=2p.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-3q+2p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=2p-3q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1215840d-bacc-5fc7-b42c-e019593a22a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1215840d-bacc-5fc7-b42c-e019593a22a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300035} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf56eb9-ff40-5f78-9ece-79037271fc6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301882} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad4509e5-63fd-5831-b1d9-cb3cfd272cb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad4509e5-63fd-5831-b1d9-cb3cfd272cb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300830} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef617c89-4f69-5572-9be1-4c4fe8cc2949" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301989}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e61f5683-d5a7-570e-8d61-a9e450d0adae" :question/math-academy-id "q-327075" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e69402e97668c50f91258ca7e8dbb5206d1cd681e73bb9333303777adc866b70.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb8209592ec6d5be435622999c1b3eb86e1756cbe9852be0efdbbf41d41777ff.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have  \n\n- $OJ=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $j$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-2j.$  \n\nSimilarly:  \n\n- $JL=|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $k$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=k.$  \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\ni=\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2j+k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j+k.$  \n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way.  Equating the coefficients, we get \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,\\mu =1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302317 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246b715e-1ea7-5159-bf5f-c1baa5326f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "246b715e-1ea7-5159-bf5f-c1baa5326f61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300137} :answer-field/id #uuid "50fdf202-df72-5566-bc56-b474f1163821" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301493} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdfd483d-4c5f-5f30-9692-eb5855d0cc50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdfd483d-4c5f-5f30-9692-eb5855d0cc50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300988} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb3a4657-18da-51c1-a9cb-2dccd31b1ae1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302030}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c302ea94-ce6a-528e-80d0-db3b0e2f4650" :question/math-academy-id "q-327078" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c1a8cde010abbeaf318a64e6c9f3b6cbd3e7bac84babbcda4c1ebfb483fa2b2.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $s=\\lambda m+\\mu n$, then$\\,\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}\nand \n$\\mu =$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "![](images/e0/e018cc5b4760b578d9ca514f9de6641e823bf5f6a8e17f019ebf24fc6ccb2248.png)\n\nIn $△OTS$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OT=4|m|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ and $m$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-4m.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $TS=2|n|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{TS}$ and $n$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{TS}=2n.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =\\overset{\\to }{OT}+\\overset{\\to }{TS} \\\\  & =-4m+2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $s=\\lambda m+\\mu n=-4m+2n.$\n\nSince $m∦n,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =2$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08c24834-8cd4-5bea-b65d-b2c5663746e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327004} {:answer/id #uuid "43f90f38-cbf5-5d7a-920b-1121b5764f3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =0$ and $\\mu =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327528} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7ece4b-a049-56ac-8617-bdfeb30b3311" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328419} {:answer/id #uuid "b21ee6fb-0023-5a59-b1cc-92b08b1e5bb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328478} {:answer/id #uuid "baf8f242-62cb-5b3c-a8e9-9e47509ff9d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :an